//? Title: Gradient Boosting: Residual Trees, Scores and Validation
//? Subtitle: Hand-checkable softmax updates and held-out Iris classification
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Classifying observations from numeric measurements requires both a decision rule and
an assessment of how it predicts unseen observations. A perfect training classification
can still have uncertain probabilities or poor generalization. Gradient boosting builds
an additive score function by fitting small trees to the current loss gradient. We
work through exact two-class and three-class updates, check a full-sample descent
bound and demonstrate an oversized step that increases loss. Iris then illustrates
learning-rate and round selection on validation rows, fixed-model decision regions,
and a final refit evaluated on reserved test rows. Validation selects rate .3 and
106 rounds; the refit correctly classifies 29 of 30 test flowers, while log loss
records the confidently mistaken prediction. All figures are local image files;
the introductory update diagram retains its editable TikZ source.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Scores turn measurements into class probabilities

For \(K\) observed training classes, let \(y_{ik}\) be one when row i has class \(k\), and zero
otherwise. Fit one score \(F_k(x)\) per class. Softmax and the mean negative log likelihood are

$$
 p_{ik}=\frac{\exp F_k(x_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^K\exp F_j(x_i)},\qquad
 L(F)=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\left[\log\sum_{k=1}^K\exp F_k(x_i)-F_{y_i}(x_i)\right].
$$
\alttext(Each probability is an exponentiated class score normalized over all classes. Mean log loss averages log-sum-exp minus the score of the observed class.)

The constant starting scores are \(F_{0,k}=\log(n_k/n)\), so the first probabilities equal
the empirical class proportions. These minimize constant-model log loss: imposing
\(\sum_k p_k=1\) on \(-\sum_k n_k\log p_k\) gives \(p_k=n_k/n\). Adding the same constant to
every score leaves softmax unchanged; scores themselves are not probabilities.
`initial_scores()` exposes the log priors and `raw_scores(data,0)` repeats them by row.

For one observation, differentiation gives \(\partial\ell_i/\partial F_{ik}=p_{ik}-y_{ik}\). Thus the negative
gradient is the pseudo-residual \(r_{ik}=y_{ik}-p_{ik}\). The global factor \(1/n\) changes the
step-size convention but not the residual-fitting direction. At each round \(m\), compute
all \(K\) targets using the same pre-round probabilities, fit a least-squares tree to each
class target, and add its shrunken prediction to that class score:

$$
 r_{imk}=y_{ik}-p_{i,m-1,k},\quad
 h_{mk}\approx\arg\min_h\sum_{i\in S_m}(r_{imk}-h(x_i))^2,\quad
 F_{mk}=F_{m-1,k}+\eta h_{mk}.
$$
\alttext(All class residuals use the probabilities before the round. A separate regression tree approximates each residual vector on the sampled rows. Its output, multiplied by learning rate eta, is added to that class score.)

`GBMClassifier` implements this direct first-order residual-tree variant. CART greedily
chooses squared-error-reducing splits and each terminal node predicts its residual mean.
It uses \(K\) trees even for two classes. There is no Newton leaf correction, class-count
correction, score recentering or line search. Consequently a numeric comparison with
scikit-learn's `GradientBoostingClassifier` would compare different algorithms: its
classification terminal updates and binary score representation differ. Friedman's
2001 paper supplies the gradient-boosting framework; the equations above specify the
particular variant used here.

Each round samples one shared set \(S_m\) without replacement for all class trees.
`subsample=1` uses all retained training rows. Otherwise its size is
`max(1,round(subsample*n))`; SplitMix64 and `seed` determine the sequence. Round count,
depth, minimum leaf size, learning rate and sampling all affect flexibility. The
constructor always fits its declared round budget; it performs no early stopping.

The diagram separates the current probabilities, the simultaneous residual targets
and the updated scores. Rendering requires LaTeX, PGF/TikZ, standalone, Latin Modern
and dvisvgm; normal documentation export consumes the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/gbm_residual_update.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/gbm_residual_update.tikz > build/gbm_residual_update.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/gbm_residual_update.svg build/gbm_residual_update.dvi > build/gbm_residual_update.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local boosting update diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local boosting update diagram.
// \image(figures/gbm_residual_update.svg)
// result_end
\caption(One boosting round uses the same pre-round probabilities for every class tree.)
\alttext(Log-prior scores feed softmax. Subtracting these probabilities from one-hot labels creates all class residuals together. Separate class trees fit those residuals; their shrunken outputs update the scores before the next softmax.)

>> When does a residual-tree step decrease loss?

With full-sample leaf means, each residual is projected onto the constants on its
chosen leaves. Within a leaf \(A\), \(\sum_{i\in A}r_i h_i=|A|\bar r_A^2=\sum_{i\in A}h_i^2\). Summing
over leaves and classes yields the exact-arithmetic identity below. The Hessian of
one-row log-sum-exp is \(\operatorname{diag}(p)-pp^T\), whose absolute row sums are \(2p_k(1-p_k)\le1/2\).
Its spectral norm is at most 1/2. Taylor's quadratic upper bound then gives

$$
 \langle r,h\rangle=\|h\|_2^2,\qquad
 L(F+\eta h)\le L(F)-\frac{\eta}{n}\langle r,h\rangle+
 \frac{\eta^2}{4n}\|h\|_2^2
 =L(F)-\frac{\eta(1-\eta/4)}n\|h\|_2^2.
$$
\alttext(For trees fitted with full-sample leaf means, the residual and update inner product equals the update squared norm. A Hessian bound of one half implies a loss decrease for a nonzero update and a learning rate strictly between zero and four.)

Thus \(0<\eta<4\) guarantees descent for a nonzero full-sample update in exact arithmetic;
implementation tolerances and rounding affect tiny changes. This is a sufficient bound,
not an optimal rate or a generalization claim. With subsampling, the leaf identity holds
on \(S_m\) and need not hold on all training rows. A large eta can overshoot even with all
rows. `step_diagnostics()` reports both inner product and squared norm over all training
rows; interpret its projection-based `quadratic_bound` as a guarantee only for full samples.

Training log loss and accuracy measure different things. Once every winning class is
correct, probability concentration can continue improving log loss while accuracy stays
flat. On new data, excessive concentration can be costly. Evaluation uses stable
log-sum-exp without clipping the true-class probability at a floor, even if softmax
underflows to zero. The historically named `training_deviance()` stores mean log loss,
without a factor of two. The multiclass Brier score is \(n^{-1}\sum_i\sum_k(p_{ik}-y_{ik})^2\),
without dividing by \(K\); smaller is better for both loss scores.

>> Contracts, cost and importance

The formula machinery fixes predictor encoding on fitting rows and omits rows with null
formula values. `kept_row_indices()` maps compact training rows back to original indices.
Predictions retain original query order, using an empty label/probability vector for a
row with null predictors and a NaN raw-score row. An empty prediction frame returns empty
predictions; a nonempty frame with no complete rows is rejected. Evaluation reports
input, retained and skipped counts, and rejects response classes absent from training.
Retained transformed predictors, learning rate and subsample must be finite. A query row
uses the frozen model whether supplied alone or in a batch. Depth and tree counts are
positive; minimum leaf size is positive and minimum split size is at least two.

For \(M\) rounds, \(K\) classes, \(p\) encoded predictors, \(n\) rows and depth limit \(D\), this implementation's
exhaustive CART splitter has a conservative \(O(MKpn^2(D+1))\) fitting bound: it scans all
candidate thresholds and repartitions rows. It is not a histogram-based booster. Staged
evaluation applies each tree once per row, \(O(MKn(D+1))\), rather than independently
refitting every prefix. Stored trees require up to \(O(MK\min(n,2^D))\) nodes plus \(O(MKn)\) stored
training targets/predictions, retained design data and sampling indices. Diagnostics
traverse fitted trees without refitting.

For each fitted class tree, normalize its featurewise squared-error gains by that tree's
total gain, or use a zero vector when it has no split. `feature_importance()` averages
these vectors equally across all \(MK\) trees. Its sum can be less than one when some trees
are constant. `importance_table()` additionally gives `relative_share`, normalized across
features to sum to one when any gain exists. Neither quantity pools raw gains across
trees or measures causality, uncertainty, permutation importance or held-out usefulness.

> Simple examples

>> Four rows: perfect classification is not probability one

Take x=(0,1,2,3), with labels (a,a,b,b). Equal priors give scores log(.5), probabilities
.5 and class-a residuals (.5,.5,−.5,−.5). A depth-one tree splits at x=1.5 and predicts
those residual means exactly; class b is the negative. At eta=.5 the first row has
scores (log(.5)+.25,log(.5)−.25). Therefore p(a)=1/(1+exp(−.5)) and mean loss is
log(1+exp(−.5)). Classification is already perfect after this single round, while the
predicted true-class probability is only about .622459.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gbm_binary.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 1, 2, 3});
  d.add_column("label", std::vector<std::string>{"a", "a", "b", "b"});
  const dm::stats::GBMClassifier m(d, "label ~ x", {20, .5, 1});
  const auto                     first  = m.predict_detail(d, 1);
  const auto                     stages = m.staged_evaluation(d);
  auto                left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> pa, pb;
  for (const auto& p : first.probability) {
    pa.push_back(p[0]);
    pb.push_back(p[1]);
  }
  left.points({0, 1, 2, 3}, pa, "class a", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .points({0, 1, 2, 3}, pb, "class b", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .line({0, 3}, {.5, .5}, "prior", {100, 116, 139})
      .title("Probabilities after one round")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("class probability")
      .y_limits(0, 1.4);
  right
      .line(
          stages.numeric_column("round").values(),
          stages.numeric_column("mean_log_loss").values(),
          "mean log loss",
          {37, 99, 235})
      .title("Loss falls after accuracy reaches one")
      .x_label("round, including prior at zero")
      .y_label("mean log loss")
      .y_limits(0, .9);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gbm_binary.svg");
  std::print(
      "Input:\n\n{}\n\nFirst-round residuals and updates:\n\n{}\n\nSelected stages:\n\n{}\n",
      d.to_md(),
      m.training_step(1)
          .select(
              {"original_row",
               "class",
               "before_probability",
               "pseudo_residual",
               "tree_value",
               "after_probability"})
          .to_md(8),
      stages.take_rows({0, 1, 5, 20})
          .select({"round", "accuracy", "mean_log_loss", "brier"})
          .to_md(8));
}
```
