> Checking the theory

>> Full-sample projection, sampling and an oversized step

Keep six x values but change labels to (a,a,b,b,a,b). The balanced prior still gives
log loss log(2). The first full-sample class-a stump predicts .5 on the first two rows
and −.25 on the remaining four; class b is its negative. Thus the squared update norm
summed over both classes is 1.5, equal to its residual inner product. With eta=.5 the
upper bound is log(2)−.5*(1−.5/4)*1.5/6, approximately .583772.

Compare that small full-sample step with sampling half the rows and with eta=20 on all
rows. The loss plot includes round zero. The diagnostic scatter places full-training
inner product on x and squared norm on y, both divided by n. Full-sample leaf means lie
on the identity; sampled leaf means need not. The oversized full-sample first step
raises loss to about 1.666697, demonstrating why arbitrary positive rates do not inherit
the descent guarantee. None of these training curves estimates held-out error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gbm_descent.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5});
  d.add_column("label", std::vector<std::string>{"a", "a", "b", "b", "a", "b"});
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame               table;
  const std::vector<std::string>       labels = {"full, eta .5", "half, eta .5", "full, eta 20"};
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {220, 38, 38}};
  double                               top = 0, extent = .05;
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 3; ++j) {
    dm::stats::GBMClassifierOptions o{12, j == 2 ? 20. : .5, 1};
    o.subsample = j == 1 ? .5 : 1.;
    o.seed      = 7;
    const dm::stats::GBMClassifier m(d, "label ~ x", o);
    const auto                     stages = m.staged_evaluation(d);
    const auto                     losses = stages.numeric_column("mean_log_loss").values();
    top = std::max(top, *std::max_element(losses.begin(), losses.end()));
    left.line(stages.numeric_column("round").values(), losses, labels[j], colors[j]);
    std::vector<double> inner, norm;
    for (std::size_t r = 1; r <= m.n_trees(); ++r) {
      const auto a = m.step_diagnostics(r);
      inner.push_back(a.double_at("gradient_tree_inner", 0) / 6);
      norm.push_back(a.double_at("tree_squared_norm", 0) / 6);
      extent = std::max({extent, std::abs(inner.back()), norm.back()});
    }
    right.points(inner, norm, labels[j], colors[j], 4);
    auto row = m.step_diagnostics(1);
    row.add_column("setting", std::vector<std::string>{labels[j]});
    table = table.ncols() ? table.concat_rows(row) : row;
  }
  left.title("A large full-sample step can raise loss")
      .x_label("round")
      .y_label("training mean log loss")
      .y_limits(0, top * 1.7);
  right
      .line(
          {-extent * .1, extent * 1.05}, {-extent * .1, extent * 1.05}, "identity", {100, 116, 139})
      .title("Leaf means and the sampled rows")
      .x_label("full-training residual inner product / n")
      .y_label("full-training squared tree norm / n")
      .x_limits(-extent * .1, extent * 1.1)
      .y_limits(-extent * .1, extent * 1.9);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gbm_descent.svg");
  std::print(
      "First-round diagnostics; bound requires full-sample projection:\n\n{}\n",
      table
          .select(
              {"setting",
               "sample_size",
               "full_sample",
               "loss_before",
               "loss_after",
               "gradient_tree_inner",
               "tree_squared_norm",
               "quadratic_bound"})
          .to_md(8));
}
```
