\alttext(The left panel plots all validation log-loss candidates for three learning rates. The right compares fitting and validation accuracy at the selected rate. Tables preview the fitting data and list the six best validation candidates, with losses and accuracies for both partitions.)

The selected candidate is eta=.3 at 106 rounds, with validation log loss .11568032
and accuracy 29/30. Its fitting accuracy is already one. The lowest validation loss
does not occur at the largest round budget, although the rate is the largest searched.
This selection is conditional on the declared grid and split.

>> Compare prefixes of the same fitted ensemble

At the selected rate, fit the 90 fitting flowers once and draw its five-round and
selected-round prefixes. Both panels reuse the same trees. Pastel grid points indicate
predicted species over synthetic coordinates; dark points are the observed fitting
flowers and identify their true species. The rectangular cells arise from axis-aligned
splits. The map is a class decision map, so a stable-looking boundary can coexist with
changing probability estimates and log loss. No test flower is used to draw or choose it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gbm_iris_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto               d = dm::datasets::iris();
  std::vector<std::size_t> fitting, validation, testing;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < d.nrows(); ++i) {
    if (i % 5 == 0)
      testing.push_back(i);
    else if (i % 5 == 1)
      validation.push_back(i);
    else
      fitting.push_back(i);
  }
  const auto train = d.take_rows(fitting), valid = d.take_rows(validation),
             test                         = d.take_rows(testing);
  const std::string               formula = "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width";
  dm::stats::GBMClassifierOptions options{120, .1, 2};
  options.min_samples_leaf = 2;
  const auto search =
      dm::stats::gbm_validation_search(train, valid, formula, options, {.03, .1, .3});
  auto o    = search.selected_options;
  o.n_trees = std::max(std::size_t(5), search.selected_rounds);
  const dm::stats::GBMClassifier m(train, formula, o);
  auto left  = m.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", 45, 5),
       right = m.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", 45, search.selected_rounds);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors  = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
  const std::vector<std::string>       species = {"setosa", "versicolor", "virginica"};
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 3; ++k) {
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < train.nrows(); ++i)
      if (train.string_at("Species", i) == species[k]) {
        x.push_back(train.double_at("Petal.Length", i));
        y.push_back(train.double_at("Petal.Width", i));
      }
    left.points(x, y, "", colors[k], 3);
    right.points(x, y, "", colors[k], 3);
  }
  left.title("Five-round prefix")
      .legend(species, colors)
      .x_label("petal length (cm)")
      .y_label("petal width (cm)")
      .x_limits(.3, 7.7)
      .y_limits(-.4, 3.8);
  right.title(std::format("Selected prefix: {} rounds", search.selected_rounds))
      .legend(species, colors)
      .x_label("petal length (cm)")
      .y_label("petal width (cm)")
      .x_limits(.3, 7.7)
      .y_limits(-.4, 3.8);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gbm_iris_regions.svg");
  std::print(
      "Metrics on the ninety fitting flowers, not held-out estimates:\n\n{}\n",
      m.evaluation(train, 5).concat_rows(m.evaluation(train, search.selected_rounds)).to_md(8));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Metrics on the ninety fitting flowers, not held-out estimates:

| round | input_rows | evaluated_rows | skipped_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 90 | 90 | 0 | 0.95555556 | 0.51846468 | 0.24803344 |
| 106 | 90 | 90 | 0 | 1.00000000 | 0.03396338 | 0.00360112 |

\image(figures/gbm_iris_regions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two prefixes share a fitted tree sequence, making the effect of additional rounds directly comparable.)
\alttext(Pastel predictions cover a regular grid in petal length and width. Dark observed points are ninety fitting flowers coloured by actual species. The panels compare five rounds with the validation-selected prefix, and the table reports fitting metrics for both.)

>> Refit once and evaluate the reserved test rows

Refit on the 120 combined fitting and validation flowers using the selected rate and
round count. Keep all tree and sampling controls fixed. The API allows a prior-only
selection: because the constructor requires a positive budget, `selected_options` then
uses one fitted round, and the explicit `selected_rounds=0` prefix supplies the selected
prior-only model. Predictions and evaluation below always pass that prefix.

Evaluate only this final model and its empirical-prior baseline on the 30 reserved test
flowers. The confusion table has actual classes in rows and predicted classes in
columns. The probability panel plots the observed class's probability for each test
flower; it is descriptive and does not establish calibration. The second panel shows
training-derived relative gain shares. These are feature-use summaries, which can
redistribute weight between correlated petal measurements. They are not evidence that
one measurement would cause a species change or improve held-out loss if retained alone.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gbm_iris_test.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto               d = dm::datasets::iris();
  std::vector<std::size_t> fitting, validation, testing;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < d.nrows(); ++i) {
    if (i % 5 == 0)
      testing.push_back(i);
    else if (i % 5 == 1)
      validation.push_back(i);
    else
      fitting.push_back(i);
  }
  const auto train = d.take_rows(fitting), valid = d.take_rows(validation),
             test                         = d.take_rows(testing);
  const std::string               formula = "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width";
  dm::stats::GBMClassifierOptions options{120, .1, 2};
  options.min_samples_leaf = 2;
  const auto search =
      dm::stats::gbm_validation_search(train, valid, formula, options, {.03, .1, .3});
  const auto                     combined = train.concat_rows(valid);
  const dm::stats::GBMClassifier final_model(combined, formula, search.selected_options);
  const auto predicted  = final_model.predict_detail(test, search.selected_rounds);
  const auto importance = final_model.importance_table();
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < final_model.classes().size(); ++k) {
    std::vector<double> x, p;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < test.nrows(); ++i)
      if (test.string_at("Species", i) == final_model.classes()[k]) {
        x.push_back(test.double_at("Petal.Length", i));
        p.push_back(predicted.probability[i][k]);
      }
    left.points(x, p, final_model.classes()[k], colors[k], 5);
  }
  left.title("Reserved test: true-class probabilities")
      .x_label("petal length (cm)")
      .y_label("probability assigned to actual species")
      .y_limits(0, 1.5);
  right
      .bars(
          {1, 2},
          importance.numeric_column("relative_share").values(),
          "relative gain share",
          {37, 99, 235},
          .6)
      .title("Final ensemble: feature use")
      .x_label("1: petal length; 2: petal width")
      .y_label("relative share")
      .y_limits(0, 1.25);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/gbm_iris_test.svg");
  std::print(
      "Final refit: {} flowers; eta {}; rounds {}.\n\nReserved-test prior baseline and selected "
      "model:\n\n{}\n\nSelected-model confusion counts:\n\n{}\n\nTraining-derived feature "
      "summaries:\n\n{}\n",
      combined.nrows(),
      search.selected_options.learning_rate,
      search.selected_rounds,
      final_model.evaluation(test, 0)
          .concat_rows(final_model.evaluation(test, search.selected_rounds))
          .to_md(8),
      final_model.confusion(test, search.selected_rounds).to_md(0),
      importance.to_md(8));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Final refit: 120 flowers; eta 0.3; rounds 106.

Reserved-test prior baseline and selected model:

| round | input_rows | evaluated_rows | skipped_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.33333333 | 1.09861229 | 0.66666667 |
| 106 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.96666667 | 0.21296239 | 0.06703447 |


Selected-model confusion counts:

| actual | pred_setosa | pred_versicolor | pred_virginica |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 10 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 9 | 1 |
| virginica | 0 | 0 | 10 |


Training-derived feature summaries:

| predictor | mean_normalized_gain | relative_share |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Petal.Length | 0.56226404 | 0.56226404 |
| Petal.Width | 0.43773596 | 0.43773596 |

\image(figures/gbm_iris_test.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Final test metrics follow validation selection and a 120-flower refit; gain shares describe the fitted trees.)
\alttext(Test flowers are plotted by petal length and probability assigned to their observed species. Bars show relative training gain shares for the two predictors. Tables report prior and selected test metrics, named confusion counts and both raw mean-normalized gains and rescaled shares.)

The final refit also classifies 29/30 test flowers correctly, with mean log loss
.21296239 and Brier score .06703447, versus the prior baseline
1.09861229 and .66666667. One versicolor is assigned to virginica; its small
true-class probability contributes substantially to the log loss. The final test
loss differs from the validation selection loss because both the fitted trees and
the evaluated observations differ. These are descriptive results for this split.

> Conclusion

The tiny examples expose the score-to-probability calculation, simultaneous multiclass
residuals and the distinction between classification accuracy and probability loss.
Full-sample leaf means satisfy a projection identity that yields a sufficient descent
bound for \(0<\eta<4\); sampling and an oversized step require separate reasoning. Training
loss reduction alone does not certify generalization.

Iris uses a declared 90/30/30 split for fitting, validation and testing, selects only by
validation log loss, and evaluates a single refitted setting on the reserved flowers.
The selected eta=.3 and 106-round model gets 29 of 30 reserved flowers correct; its
mean test log loss .21296239 still records the confidently mistaken flower. These
selection, final-loss and confusion results describe this split and grid.
A larger dataset, repeated outer evaluation and calibration assessment would answer
questions that these thirty test rows and training-derived gain shares cannot.

> References

- J. H. Friedman (2001), “Greedy function approximation: A gradient boosting machine”,
  Annals of Statistics 29(5), 1189–1232. https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1013203451.
  The gradient-boosting framework; this report explicitly uses direct residual leaf means.
- Scikit-learn, “Gradient Boosting”, official ensemble documentation:
  https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/ensemble.html#gradient-boosting.
  Its classification leaf updates and binary score convention differ from this implementation.
- R. A. Fisher (1936), “The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems”,
  Annals of Eugenics 7(2), 179–188. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x.

